{{smaller block|Cast not thyne eyes to ne yet fro,
As thou werte full of ''toyes.''
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 80.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Ta.'' Has he never been courtier, my lord?
''Mo.'' Never, my lady.
''Be.'' And why did the ''toy'' take him in th' head now?
{{right|''Chapman,'' Bussy D'Ambois, i. 1.}}}}

7. Same as ''toy-mutch.'' [Now Scotch.]

{{smaller block|On my head no ''toy''
But was her pattern.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Two Noble Kinsmen, i. 3.}}}}

8. In ''music,'' in old English writers, a dance-tune or other light, trifling piece.—9. A toy dog.

{{smaller block|In the ''Toys'' equal first went to the well-known Wee Flower and a very good Black-and-tan called Little Jem.
{{right|''The Field'' (London), Jan. 28, 1882. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Philosophical toy,''' any device or contrivance, of no practical use, which serves to illustrate some fact or principle in natural science in an attractive or entertaining as well as instructive manner, as a contrivance for producing the effects of so-called natural magic. The bottle-imp is a good example. See cuts under ''Cartesian'' and ''phenakistoscope.''—'''Steel toys.''' See ''steel.''—'''To take toy†,''' to become restive; start.}}

{{smaller block|The hot horse, hot as fire,
''Took toy'' at this, and fell to what disorder
His power could give his will, bounds, comes on end.
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''), Two Noble Kinsmen, v. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Toy dog,''' any dog bred to an unusually small or pygmy size and kept as a pet or plaything; a toy. Spaniels and terriers are so bred in some strains, and there are various mongrel toys.—'''Toy spaniel.''' See ''spaniel,'' 1.—'''Toy terrier,''' a terrier bred to small or pygmy size and kept as a plaything. Such terriers are usually of the black-and-tan variety, and some of them are among the smallest dogs known.}}

{{smaller block|In-breeding is certain, if carried too far, to stunt the growth of any animal, and this is, without any doubt, the means by which the modern ''toy-terrier'' was first originated.
{{right|''V. Shaw,'' Book of the Dog, xxii.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''toy''' (toi), ''v.'' [< ''toy'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To trifle; amuse one's self; play.

{{smaller block|Some plaid with strawes; some ydly satt at ease;<br>But other some could not abide to ''toy''.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. ix. 35.}}
Pale dreamers, whose fantastic lay<br>''Toys'' with smooth trifles like a child at play.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Poetry.}}}}

'''2.''' To dally amorously.

{{smaller block|Aft on the banks we'd sit us thair,<br>And sweetly kiss and ''toy''.
{{right|''Gilderoy'' (Child's Ballads, VI. 199).}}
A roi fainéant who chewed bang, and ''toyed'' with dancing girls.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Warren Hastings.}}}}

'''To tick and toy†.''' {{smaller|See ''tick''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''II.'''† ''trans.'' To treat in playful fashion; play with.

{{smaller block|They must have oyle, candles, wine and water, flowre, and such other things trifled and ''toyed'' withal.
{{right|''Dering'', Expos. on Heb. iii.}}}}

'''toy-block''' (toi′blok), ''n.'' One of a set of small blocks, usually of wood or papier-mâché, variously shaped, and plain, lettered, or pictured, forming a plaything for children.

'''toy-box''' (toi′boks), ''n.'' A box for holding toys; a box of toys. ''Carlyle'', Sartor Resartus, ii. 6.

'''to-year''' (tọ̈-yēr′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''toyere''; orig. two words: see ''to''<sup>1</sup> and ''year''. Cf. ''to-day''.] In this year; during the year: often pronounced ''t' year''. ''Grose.'' [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|Yive hem joye that hit here<br>Of alle that they dreme ''to-yere''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 84.}}}}

'''toyer''' (toi′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''toy'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who toys; one who is full of idle tricks.

