{{smaller block|Spinola.{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. v. 6.}}}}

{{smaller block|We firmly believe that no British government has ever deviated from that line of internal policy which he [Lord Holland] has ''traced'', without detriment to the public.{{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Holland.}}}}

'''2.''' To write, especially by a careful or laborious formation of the letters; form in writing.

{{smaller block|Every letter I ''trace'' tells me with what rapidity Life follows my pen.{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, ix. 8.}}}}

{{smaller block|The signature of another plainly appeared to have been ''traced'' by a hand shaking with emotion.{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xiv.}}}}

Specifically—'''3.''' To copy, as a drawing or engraving, by following the lines and marking them on a superimposed sheet, through which they appear.

{{smaller block|There is an inscription round the inside of the [bronze] vase, which was ''traced'' of, as it is engraved on it, and shews exactly the circumference of the vase.{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. ii. 207.}}}}

'''4.''' To cover with traced lines, as with writing or tracery. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The deep-set windows, stain'd and ''traced'',
Would seem slow-flaming crimson fires
From shadow'd grots of arches interlaced.{{right|''Tennyson'', Palace of Art.}}}}

{{smaller block|And he holds a palm-leaf scroll in his hands,
''Traced'' with the Prophet's wise commands.{{right|''Whittier'', The Palm-Tree.}}}}

'''5.''' To follow the track, trail, or path of; pursue: a general term, the verbs ''track'' and ''trail'' being more specific, as in hunting.
